WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.398 --> 00:00:02.440
LEIGHA JARETT: If you
watched our previous video,

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:05.980
you're already familiar with
how BigQuery stores your data.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.590
But how does BigQuery
actually process your data

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.600
and execute queries?

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.820
Understanding what goes
on behind the scenes

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.990
is key to ensuring your
queries will run super fast.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.690
In this video, we'll walk
through the life of a query

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.480
and show you how to leverage
the query plan to troubleshoot

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.880
and optimize your SQL.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.212
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.130
Let's begin with an overview
of BigQuery's architecture.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.280
In our last video, we talked
about Google's distributed file

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.760
system that stores
BigQuery data, Colossus.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.040
But to actually
compute data, BigQuery

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.620
uses Dremel, the query engine.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:49.920
Here, workers or slots, like
we discussed in our video

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.080
on jobs and the
reservation model,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.330
are used to extract
data from storage

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.580
and perform aggregations.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.980
To increase the performance,
each of these slots

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.710
does work independently
from one another.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.410
Instead of communicating
directly with each other,

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.110
they use what we call
the remote memory shuffle

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.480
to store intermediary results.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.020
In BigQuery, each shuffled
row can be consumed by slots

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.210
as soon as it's
created, which makes

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.220
it possible to
execute distributed

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.560
operations in a pipeline.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.750
All of this data
movement is facilitated

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.920
by Jupiter our super-fast,
Google-scale network.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.140
Now that you understand
the architecture,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.960
let's see what happens inside
of BigQuery when you actually

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.420
submit a query to be run.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.640
First off, an API
request will be

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.280
sent with all the
details of the query.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.220
To proceed, there's some
level of API processing

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.640
that must occur--

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.050
for example, authenticating
and authorizing the request,

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.090
and building and
tracking metadata

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:53.820
such as the SQL statement, cloud
project, and query parameters.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:56.910
Next, BigQuery will
perform lexing and parsing.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:02:00.510
Lexing refers to the process
of scanning an array of bytes--

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.970
in this case, the
raw SQL statement--

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.420
and converting that
into a series of tokens.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.229
Parsing is the process of
consuming those tokens to build

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:13.710
up a syntactical tree
representation of the query,

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.750
which can be validated
and understood

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.180
by BigQuery's software.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.490
While lexing and
parsing are underway,

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.240
BigQuery will also perform
catalog resolution.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.120
This basically
means that BigQuery

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.340
will resolve references to
entities like tables, views,

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.010
procedures, and functions.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:36.000
As a more fully-formed picture
of the request emerges,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.360
BigQuery begins to
build a query plan.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.640
Here, BigQuery will use
different techniques

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.160
to refactor and
improve the query,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.090
including adapting it to run as
a set of distributed execution

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:49.470
tasks.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.850
Once BigQuery has
the plan in place,

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:55.890
it will work through the query
stages in the execution graph.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.860
Besides the slots that perform
the work of the query plan

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:02.010
itself, additional
slots monitor and direct

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.410
the overall progress.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.300
Not only will these
slots determine

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.760
how the work is
queued and executed,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.060
but they can also leverage
BigQuery's dynamic planning

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.820
capabilities to make
on-the-fly adjustments

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.240
to the query plan that will
further optimize performance.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.930
Finally, when the query
has finished executing,

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:25.680
the results will be sent
back to you, the user.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:26.220
Awesome.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.050
So now that you have
a picture of what

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.330
happens behind the scenes
when a query is submitted,

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.140
let's go a little bit deeper
and see what one of those query

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.110
plans actually looks like.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.510
Here, I'm going to
run a simple query

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:41.370
to count the number
of trips that

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.000
began at a station with Broadway
in the name against the Citi

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.230
Bike data available in
BigQuery's public data sets.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.170
In this first stage of the
query plan, a set of slots

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:56.370
will access the distributed
storage to read in the table.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.500
The input table is
actually made up

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.110
of a set of input file blocks.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.860
For each input block, a slot
is needed to process the file,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.980
read, and filter down the rows.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.020
Each worker then
writes one record

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.900
into shuffle that contains the
partial count for that input

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.920
file.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.579
This second stage here reads
from those shuffle records

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:21.420
as its input and then
sums them together.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:24.850
It then writes the output
file into a single file,

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:28.860
which becomes accessible as
the results of the query.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.030
In the console, after
you run a query,

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:35.090
you can view the query
plan to see this in action.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:38.780
In the execution details, we
can see the average and maximum

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:42.230
times each worker spent in the
wait phase, where the engine is

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:44.750
waiting for either slots
to become available

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.750
or for a previous stage to
start writing results that it

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.730
can begin consuming.

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:52.580
In the read phase, where
the worker is reading data,

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.820
either from Colossus
or from shuffle.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.550
In the compute phase,
where the actual processing

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:03.620
takes place, such as evaluating
SQL functions or expressions.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:05.930
And in the write phase,
where the shuffle

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.880
output is written
in the next stage

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.940
or in the final output table.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.160
Using the query
execution details,

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.340
we can clearly see the different
stages of the query plan,

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:20.060
like we showed before, where
the first stage includes workers

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.430
that read, filter, and
aggregate the data,

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.780
and then write the
results to the shuffle.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.510
And in the second stage,
we read from the shuffle,

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:30.640
aggregate the partial
results, and then

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.640
write out the final results.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:36.280
In addition to being
available in the console,

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.790
the query plan information can
be retrieved through the API

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:43.180
from the job response or
by querying the information

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.930
schema.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:48.250
So what can you actually
do with this information?

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:51.160
First off, the query plan
and timeline statistics

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.400
can help you understand
whether certain stages dominate

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.560
resource utilization.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:59.800
For example, a join stage
that generates far more output

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.830
rows than input rows can
indicate an opportunity

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.730
to filter early in that query.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.910
Additionally, if you notice
a significant difference

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:12.750
between average and maximum
time spent by workers,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.410
that could indicate data skew,
which results in one worker

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.770
having much more data to
process than the others.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:24.810
That can, again, represent an
opportunity to filter early.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.320
Or if there is a lot of
time spent on CPU tasks,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.710
you may want to consider
approximate functions, which

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:35.970
are often within 1%
of the exact function.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:38.700
Armed with your new
understanding of how BigQuery

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.030
executes queries, you're now
ready to dig in and start

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:45.150
troubleshooting your
own SQL statements.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.610
For more details on
specific strategies

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.860
on how to optimize
your SQL queries,

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.780
stay tuned for our next video.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.510
Thanks for tuning
in to this episode

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:57.420
of "BigQuery Spotlight,"
and remember, stay curious.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:00.770
[MUSIC PLAYING]

